---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: genesis-plans-legacy-routing
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  The first plans home: about two hundred plans and designs written between 2026-03 and 2026-04,
  superseded by genesis/docs/superpowers/plans/. The files stay in place as a record. The
  p2p-plan-audit hook and the historian agent still read them. New plans are routed to the
  governed home at birth.
rules:
  - id: route-plans-to-governed-home
    class: dispatch
    when: { write: "*.md", new: true }
    route-to: { dest: genesis/docs/superpowers/plans/ }
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: librarian
      dispatch-prompt: >
        Confirm whether this is genuinely a new plan and name the governed destination or an
        existing artifact to merge into. Report ROUTE, MERGE, or DROP to the editing agent; do not
        move or create files and do not derail implementation.
    why: >
      New plans go to genesis/docs/superpowers/plans/, born with id, status and cites under the
      lifecycle gate, and a design spec goes to genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/. This directory is
      the retired first plans home, not a live one.
---
# genesis/plans — the first plans home (legacy)

This is the same routing as `genesis/docs/plans/.epr-meta`, which cut births in that legacy
directory to zero. Existing plans are left where they are, because tooling reads them by path:
`.claude/hooks/p2p-plan-audit.py` scans `genesis/plans/*.md`, and the historian agent treats the
directory as the historical plans wing. Editing an existing plan is never routed; only a new file
is.

`covers: subtree` is the ownership claim. The rule is written inline in both legacy directories;
its graduated form is one registry policy that both bind.
